# The left operand gives an operation its type, so a float literal on the
# right of an integer literal lands where an integer is taken. It converts to
# the float types alone, and the refusal says so, naming the value and the
# type: it named a "type specifier" that the expression does not have.
main: function is {
    a: 1 + 0.5
    b: 1 / 3.0
    print "\{a} \{b}"
}
